---
const peers = [
  {
    name: 'Internet Computer',
    note: 'Same ambition, opposite center. ICP hosts WASM backends on subnets, thirteen nodes for a standard application subnet and a few dozen for the ones carrying stronger guarantees, and it scales by adding subnets. The price is that a call from one subnet to another is asynchronous and non-atomic, so a failure halfway through leaves the developer to write the compensation. BluePods keeps one global DAG, so a transaction stays atomic across its objects whoever holds them and nothing rolls back. It pays for that in throughput.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Sui',
    note: 'The closest cousin. The object model, version-based conflict detection, and the DAG lineage all come from this family. Sui finalizes single-owner transactions on a distinct, faster path, so the guarantee is not uniform across operations, and it sends large data to a separate network with its own token (Walrus). BluePods keeps storage and execution inside one protocol and one order, with no separate network or token.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Solana',
    note: 'The same bandwidth bet, a different answer. Solana is leader-based, and every validator holds and executes the whole state. BluePods is leaderless and scales the set of machines a transaction needs instead, asking only the ten to fifty validators that hold its objects.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ethereum',
    note: 'An account model over one global state commitment. Every validator processes every transaction, and capacity beyond that lives in an ecosystem of separate layer 2 execution domains; composing across them is asynchronous and non-atomic, the same failure mode as the subnets above. BluePods decouples unrelated state into objects with holders of their own, and pays for it in composability: the per-transaction reference cap bounds how many objects one step can touch, so the deep multi-contract compositions Ethereum allows are harder here.',
  },
];
---

<section class="band" id="standing">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <div class="head">
      <div>
        <h2>Against the neighbours.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="aside">
        Three of them scale by making the machine cheaper. One splits the order into
        subnets. BluePods splits which machines hold what, and keeps one order over all
        of them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rows">
      {
        peers.map((peer) => (
          <article class="row">
            <h3>{peer.name}</h3>
            <p>{peer.note}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .aside {
    max-width: 28ch;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .rows {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }

  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 2fr);
    gap: 1rem clamp(2rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    padding-block: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .row p {
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
